# Keep Docs Current

When a change alters **user-visible** product behavior, update the matching docs in the **same** change set. Do not leave README, `examples/`, or `docs/` stale.

## When this applies

Trigger on changes to (non-exhaustive):

- CLI flags, subcommands, exit behavior, or printed outcomes (`cmd/skilleval`)
- Eval YAML schema or expects semantics (`eval/`)
- Runner credentials, prerequisites, or normalized observables authors rely on (`runner/`)
- Checker pass/fail semantics that docs describe (`checker/`)
- Install, build, or env setup assumptions

## Required follow-through

In the same PR / commit series:

- Update `README.md` (and any `docs/**` user guides) so install, authoring, run, credentials, and examples stay accurate
- Update `examples/` when the public eval contract or a worked example would otherwise mislead newcomers
- Prefer docs in the same logical commit as the behavior change, or an immediately following `docs:` commit — not a later cleanup

## When not to rewrite docs

- Pure internal refactors with no user-facing contract change
- Inventing a full expects / tool catalog when only an example-level note is needed
